<AashaCare />
<div class="seo-answer"><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Aasha Child Development &amp; Early Intervention Centre in Greater Noida supports families seeking child-development guidance for communication, learning, attention, behaviour, sensory experiences, and developmental differences.</div><section class="seo-hero"><p class="seo-kicker">Children, adolescents, and families</p><h1>Child-development care that includes the family</h1><p class="seo-lead">Families can seek guidance for communication, learning, attention, behaviour, sensory experiences, or developmental differences. The first step is understanding the child’s context.</p><div class="seo-actions"><a class="btn" href="/aasha/book">Request an Aasha appointment</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/aasha">Read about Aasha</a></div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>What families may explore</h2><div class="seo-grid"><article class="seo-card"><h3>Development and communication</h3><p>Concerns about speech, language, social communication, play, or early milestones can be discussed.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Attention and learning</h3><p>School concerns, ADHD questions, learning differences, and practical classroom or home strategies may be explored.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Behaviour and regulation</h3><p>Families can talk about routines, emotional regulation, sensory needs, sleep, behaviour, and parenting support.</p></article></div><div class="seo-note"><strong>Assessment note:</strong> An online checklist cannot diagnose autism, ADHD, or a learning disability. A clinician decides what assessment or referral is appropriate.</div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>Helpful next steps</h2><p>See <a href="/adhd-autism-assessment">ADHD and autism assessment information</a>, <a href="/learning-disability-assessment">learning assessment information</a>, or <a href="/faq">common questions</a>.</p></section>
  <section class="seo-section"><h2>Support through Aasha</h2><p><a href="/aasha#services">Explore Aasha’s therapies</a>. Our Greater Noida team can help families discuss appropriate next steps. <a href="/aasha#contact">Contact Aasha for an assessment</a>.</p></section>
<section class="seo-section"><h2>How to choose a first child-development enquiry</h2><p>Families do not need to identify a diagnosis before contacting Aasha. Describe one or two situations that prompted concern, what your child is trying to do, what makes it easier or harder, and what support has already been tried. Strengths and interests are useful context too. If a skill has been lost after being used consistently, mention when you first noticed the change so the team can advise how promptly the child should be seen.</p><p>For communication concerns, including understanding and expression, see <a href="/aasha/speech-language-therapy">speech and language support</a>. For schoolwork or learning questions, review <a href="/learning-disability-assessment">learning assessment</a> and <a href="/aasha/special-education">special education</a>. For developmental or diagnostic questions, read <a href="/aasha/developmental-assessment">Aasha assessment information</a>. Families can begin with a concern even when it is not clear which service fits.</p><h2>What happens from intake to recommendations?</h2><ol><li><strong>Initial enquiry:</strong> Share the child’s age, a brief description of the concern and your preferred contact method. Use Aasha’s booking route and avoid sending a detailed clinical history through a general form.</li><li><strong>First conversation:</strong> The team clarifies the family’s questions and explains which service or clinician may be relevant. You can ask what to bring and whether school information is useful.</li><li><strong>Assessment or service planning:</strong> If assessment is appropriate, the clinician explains the question, methods and likely process. If a therapy service is the right start, goals are discussed with the family.</li><li><strong>Feedback and next steps:</strong> Ask how findings, recommendations or a written report will be shared and whether a separate feedback appointment is included. Plans can change as the team understands the child better.</li></ol><p>The number of visits and final deliverables depend on the concern and selected pathway. Confirm appointment count, fee, written report and feedback arrangements with Aasha before booking; do not assume every pathway includes the same package.</p><h2>Parents and caregivers as partners</h2><p>Parents can bring reports or examples from home and school if they are available, but should not delay contact while collecting a complete file. The team may ask how communication, sleep, play, transitions, eating, learning or daily routines look in different settings. If coordination with a school or another clinician would help, the family can discuss what information to share and provide consent where required.</p><p>A child’s needs may look different across home, school and clinic. Concrete examples are often more useful than a label: when does a task become difficult, what helps, and what does the child enjoy? Aasha’s approach is to understand the child’s strengths and needs and agree practical goals with the family, not to make a child appear typical or remove harmless differences.</p><h2>Book through the child-care pathway</h2><p>All child-related care on this website is coordinated through <a href="/aasha">Aasha Early Intervention Centre</a>. Use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha appointment requests</a> or its published phone number to ask about an initial visit. Mind Grace adult psychiatry uses a separate <a href="/book">adult booking page</a>. A request is not a confirmed slot; the team will follow up to discuss availability and agree a time.</p><p>Review Aasha’s <a href="/aasha/therapy-costs">therapy and assessment cost information</a>, its address and hours on the <a href="/aasha#contact">Aasha page</a>, and the broader <a href="/process">first-visit guide</a>. A developmental check-in on this site is a discussion aid only, not a diagnostic or validated screening test. For immediate danger, use emergency services rather than waiting for routine coordination.</p></section></main>
